---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { scales, path, methods } from '../data/site';
---

<Base title="Research">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><b>●</b> Research interests</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Diasporic<br />spatiotemporal<br />genomics</h1>
      <p>
        Using pigmentation as a model for how history and admixture change the trajectory of human variation, read
        from historical and modern genomes together with the written record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <p class="big-quote">
        I am a <em>genetic historian.</em> I read DNA as a historical document.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          As a genetic historian, I use DNA to study human history, with a particular interest in what
          happens when populations that were once separated come together. Human populations have always
          migrated and admixed, but as globalization accelerates these processes, understanding how
          admixture reshapes human variation has become increasingly important. My research seeks to
          understand not only how populations come together, but also how the biological legacies of those
          encounters unfold over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          I approach these questions through the study of skin pigmentation, a trait that offers a
          particularly powerful window into human variation. Pigmentation is among the most variable and
          evolutionarily dynamic traits in our species. Its genetic architecture is relatively well
          characterized, it has been shaped by natural selection across diverse environments, and it remains
          responsive to environmental conditions within an individual’s lifetime. This combination makes
          pigmentation an ideal model for studying human variation across multiple scales: from differences
          between populations to variation within them, and from evolutionary histories spanning thousands
          of years to biological responses unfolding within a single lifetime. By following pigmentation
          across populations with different histories of admixture, I can investigate how human variation is
          reshaped as previously separated populations come together.
        </p>
        <p>
          Understanding these changes, however, requires more than genetics alone. What makes my approach as
          much historical as it is genetic is my treatment of DNA as a historical document, one that must be
          read alongside the written record. Throughout history, societies have constructed and enforced
          very different ideas about ancestry and racial belonging, with profound consequences for how
          populations interacted and formed families. In the United States, for example, the one-drop rule
          classified anyone with any known African ancestry as Black, while caste systems in parts of the
          Caribbean and South America distinguished individuals according to their degree of African
          ancestry. These systems did more than define social categories: they shaped patterns of marriage,
          reproduction, and gene flow, potentially setting populations on very different trajectories of
          human variation. By bringing historical and contemporary genomes into conversation with laws,
          legal codes, and archival records, I aim to understand how these social systems became embedded in
          the biological histories of populations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading genomes as archives of human history also requires developing better tools for
          interpreting what they contain. Many methods for reconstructing population history were designed
          around groups that have been separated for long periods, making them less reliable when applied to
          highly admixed populations or those that have diverged only recently. An important component of my
          research therefore involves developing and refining computational approaches that can better
          capture how genetic variation changes through time, particularly in populations with complex
          histories of admixture. I also incorporate epigenomic data to investigate another dimension of
          these histories: biological plasticity, or how individuals respond to the environments in which
          they live. Together, these approaches allow me to examine not only the genetic legacies of past
          population encounters, but also how human biology continues to respond to changing social and
          environmental conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ultimately, my goal is to build an integrative research program that brings genetics, history, and
          the environment together to understand how human variation is shaped, transformed, and transmitted
          across generations. I view genomes not simply as records of biological ancestry, but as archives
          of human movement, social structures, and environmental experience. By reading these archives
          alongside the historical record and the environments in which people live, I hope to tell a fuller
          story of where human variation comes from, how it continues to change, and where it may be headed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Three scales</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="scales">
        {
          scales.map((s) => (
            <article class="scale">
              <div class="num">{s.n}</div>
              <div class="kicker">{s.kicker}</div>
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The path here</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="path">
        {
          path.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>{p.place}</h3>
                <div class="what">{p.what}</div>
              </div>
              <p>{p.detail}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <h2>Methods I use and build</h2>
      <ul class="chips">
        {methods.map((m) => <li class="plain">{m}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
